    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d258">

                <pb facs="0440" n="344" xml:id="pg344"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Jackson to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1898-12-14"><hi rendition="#italic">December 14,
                        1898</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 663.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have much pleasure in transmitting
                    herewith a copy and translation of a note which has just been received from the
                    Imperial foreign office, in which I am informed that a less stringent
                    examination for the San Jose scale is in future to be made in the case of
                    American dried prunes imported into Germany. Although this may be of little
                    importance in itself, it is satisfactory as indicating the good will of the
                    Imperial Government, as well as the bona fides of the experts on whose opinions
                    Count Possadowsky recently said the Government would base its future action
                    regarding the treatment of American fruit.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d258-a1.xml" xpointer="d258-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
